# Profile: phase2
# The durable write ledger, the authority store, scoped credentials and enforced route policy, all on at
# once, for the THROWAWAY gate stack only. Product defaults are never changed by this file.
#
# requires: compose
#
# Restart: its own. NOT part of the flags-on union restart: NS_POLICY_MODE=enforce denies every identity the
# authority store does not know, which would break every other profile's scenarios on the same stack.
#
# Bootstrap the gate must do around this profile (documented in acceptance/PHASE2_STORIES.md):
#   1. run the `default` profile first with a fixed NS_RUN_ID, so the acceptance users and their pools exist;
#   2. before the restart, in the API container, bind those users and publish their grants:
#        python -m policy_migration apply --tenant acc --authority-sqlite /data/ingest/p2-authority.db \
#          --qdrant-url http://qdrant:6333 --collection neuralscape_memories --no-legacy-dictator-grants
#   3. restart the stack with this file's lines on the API and every worker, then run this profile with the
#      SAME NS_RUN_ID. Do not write data as the `acc-<runid>-outsider` identity in step 1.
# The SQLite files live on the API's `ingest_data` volume (/data/ingest), the one volume that is writable by
# the service user and survives the restart. The ledger is a single-host SQLite file: when the write path
# starts using it, the worker that applies intents must mount the same volume.

LEDGER_ENABLED=true
LEDGER_BACKEND=sqlite
LEDGER_SQLITE_PATH=/data/ingest/p2-ledger.db

AUTHORITY_ENABLED=true
AUTHORITY_BACKEND=sqlite
AUTHORITY_SQLITE_PATH=/data/ingest/p2-authority.db

NS_POLICY_MODE=enforce
CREDENTIALS_ENABLED=true
